{"type":"message","id":"ff766322","parentId":"dcaa293c","timestamp":"2026-03-29T00:59:11.203Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"继续给 #56683 写评论：","thinkingSignature":"6da975bdda639241b04376e1bd583a1f3b33d5c42e7077c4dfa0b86e55e2a0be"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_a4lggiuyijup_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace && gh pr comment 56683 --body \"## PR Review: Fix 401/403 misclassified as rate limits\n\n**Approve.** The fix correctly addresses the root cause: HTTP status takes precedence over message content for classification.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Prioritizing explicit HTTP status over message keywords is the right approach — a 401/403 response body might contain \\\"quota\\\" or \\\"rate limit\\\" in the error message from the upstream provider, but the HTTP status is the authoritative signal\n- Routing auth failures to the \\`disabled\\` lane instead of \\`cooldown\\` lane is correct — auth errors aren't transient rate limits\n- Keeping 429 behavior unchanged is important for backward compatibility\n- The regression tests added for \\`HTTP 401 ... insufficient quota → auth\\` and \\`HTTP 403 ... access denied → auth\\` are good coverage\n\n**One clarification:** \\\"This does not introduce permanent disablement\\\" is correctly noted — the existing \\`disabledUntil\\` window still expires and profiles become eligible again. The fix only changes which lane they enter, not the recovery mechanism.\n\n**LGTM.**\n\n---\n*Reviewed as part of OpenClaw community contribution tracking*\" --repo openclaw/openclaw 2>&1","timeout":30}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":79202,"output":302,"cacheRead":22797,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":102301,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774745943133,"responseId":"0617ac57dc445a06a97c436129f3ef09"}}
